60,3,1,talk,Misaki,,Anchor has engineers on site. That means someone is there when the supervisors get confused. The others rely on remote support.,"{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Kenji,,"Anchor is on our servers. If the yard devices fail, the engineers can fix the integration locally. Corvus is too modular; it will break.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Toshio,,Corvus is modular. That means separate licenses. That means separate add-ons. That means the board will refuse to pay for the next one.,"{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Yumi,,"Meridian is fixed. If we add a new account, we can’t change the rules. Anchor is built for transport, so it should handle our specific needs.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,Tessera uses a partner for migration. That’s exactly what failed in 2018. Anchor has their own engineers. That’s a direct line.,"{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Misaki,,"@Benjamin, I agree. The partner model is risky. Anchor’s on-site team can actually teach the supervisors, not just send a manual.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Kenji,,"@Toshio, I know the cost is higher, but if Corvus breaks during peak season, the cost of lost freight is higher.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Toshio,,"@Kenji, I hear you. But if Anchor’s on-site team leaves after rollout, who is there? The board won't approve a permanent retainer.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Yumi,,"@Misaki, I like that Anchor is specific. But if the system is too rigid, we’ll hit the same wall when the business changes.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Yumi, Anchor is built for transport. It’s not a generic suite. It should handle the specific data structures we have.","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[""Yumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Misaki,,I’m leaning toward Anchor. The on-site support is the only thing that will keep the supervisors engaged.,"{""words"":17,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Kenji,,I’m voting for Anchor. It’s the only one that can handle the physical reality of our depots and the peak season.,"{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Toshio,,"I’m voting for Anchor. It’s the only one with a clear, single-vendor cost structure. No hidden add-ons.","{""words"":17,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Yumi,,I’m voting for Anchor. It’s the only one where someone is physically present to take ownership when things break.,"{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,I’m voting for Anchor. It’s the only one with a direct engineering team for the data migration. No external partners.,"{""words"":20,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Misaki,,I’m done. The human factor is the deciding one.,"{""words"":9,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Kenji,,I’m done. The physical factor is the deciding one.,"{""words"":9,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
